{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,180p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && git -C /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal status --short | head\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_52","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,180p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md && git -C /srv/hwebench/clones/gpt-6-luna_xhigh-v2-rep3/formal/riscv-formal status --short | head\"","aggregated_output":"# Iterative divider implementation notes\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a shared 32-step restoring divider in `rtl/ex_stage.sv`. It captures the forwarded operands and signed/remainder mode, then holds its result until EX accepts it.\n- Added an EX interlock. While division is in progress, IF and ID hold, EX/MEM receives a bubble, and older instructions continue through MEM/WB. A completed result remains parked while an older data-memory transaction is stalled.\n- Retained the forwarded divide operands for the final EX/MEM RVFI operand fields. Without this, forwarding sources can leave the pipeline during the 32-cycle operation even though the divider has already captured the correct values.\n- Added an ALU parameter that disables combinational divide/remainder logic in the core's EX instance. The default keeps the combinational ALU behavior for the existing standalone ALU tests. `RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS` still uses the existing formal stand-ins without starting the divider.\n- Added cycle-level divider tests covering signed and unsigned quotient/remainder, zero divisors, signed overflow, directed vectors, and randomized operands.\n\n## Deviations and concerns\n\n- The divider module is colocated in `ex_stage.sv` because the formal source list is fixed and explicitly includes that file; a separate `divider.sv` would not be read by the formal wrapper without changing a protected config.\n- Divide and remainder instructions take 32 iterations, plus pipeline entry and completion cycles. Other instructions retain their existing throughput.\n- The raw lint command that expands `rtl/*.sv` lexically places `core.sv` before `core_pkg.sv`, despite the RTL requiring `core_pkg.sv` first. The package-first project lint target passes.\n- Gowin timing reports the new worst path as register-file read through ID/EX, EX/IF and hazard logic to the ID/EX operand register (27.99 ns, 27 cells). No divider path appears among the five worst paths.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `make lint TARGET=bench`: pass.\n- `make test TARGET=bench`: 6 passed, including the new iterative divider suite.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: 53 passed, 0 failed. The run auto-selected the nret=1 wrapper and checks.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl OBJ_DIR=cores/bench/obj_dir NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass for both ELF traces, including random instruction/data stalls and CoreMark CRC checks.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: place option 0 Fmax 44.062 MHz; worst path 27.99 ns.\n?? cores/bench-w1NdubXVl/\n?? cores/bench-w2FLZ357M/\n?? cores/bench-w3lRqn8nL/\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
