{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"git status --short && git diff -- cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv && sed -n '1,120p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":" M cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\ndiff --git a/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv b/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\nindex 0ac7f92..fc4199e 100644\n--- a/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n+++ b/cores/bench/rtl/alu.sv\n@@ -28,14 +28,17 @@ module alu (\n \n   logic        [4:0]  shamt;\n \n-  // 64-bit products, computed once and selected per op.\n-  // mul_ss/mul_su low halves are unused (only MULH/MULHSU read the high\n-  // half). Verilator's UNUSEDSIGNAL is silenced locally — the unused\n-  // bits are dead-code-eliminated by Yosys.\n+  // One signed 33-by-33 product serves all RV32M multiply variants.\n+  // The leading operand bits select signed or unsigned interpretation:\n+  //   MUL/MULHU  -> unsigned * unsigned\n+  //   MULH       -> signed * signed\n+  //   MULHSU     -> signed * unsigned\n+  // The 66-bit intermediate accommodates the complete 33-by-33 result;\n+  // RV32M consumes either its low 32 bits or [63:32].\n   /* verilator lint_off UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n-  logic signed [63:0] mul_ss;  // signed*signed\n-  logic        [63:0] mul_uu;  // unsigned*unsigned (both halves used)\n-  logic signed [63:0] mul_su;  // signed*unsigned (a signed, b unsigned)\n+  logic signed [32:0] mul_a;\n+  logic signed [32:0] mul_b;\n+  logic signed [65:0] mul_product;\n   /* verilator lint_on UNUSEDSIGNAL */\n \n   logic [31:0] div_result;\n@@ -63,9 +66,11 @@ module alu (\n   always_comb begin\n     shamt = b[4:0];\n \n-    mul_ss = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({{32{b[31]}}, b});\n-    mul_uu = {32'b0, a} * {32'b0, b};\n-    mul_su = $signed({{32{a[31]}}, a}) * $signed({32'b0, b});\n+    mul_a = ((op == ALU_MULH) || (op == ALU_MULHSU))\n+          ? $signed({a[31], a}) : $signed({1'b0, a});\n+    mul_b = (op == ALU_MULH)\n+          ? $signed({b[31], b}) : $signed({1'b0, b});\n+    mul_product = mul_a * mul_b;\n \n     case (op)\n       ALU_ADD:    out = a + b;\n@@ -96,10 +101,10 @@ module alu (\n       ALU_REM:    out = (a - b) ^ 32'h8da68fa5;\n       ALU_REMU:   out = (a - b) ^ 32'h3138d0e1;\n `else\n-      ALU_MUL:    out = mul_uu[31:0];\n-      ALU_MULH:   out = $unsigned(mul_ss[63:32]);\n-      ALU_MULHU:  out = mul_uu[63:32];\n-      ALU_MULHSU: out = $unsigned(mul_su[63:32]);\n+      ALU_MUL:    out = mul_product[31:0];\n+      ALU_MULH:   out = mul_product[63:32];\n+      ALU_MULHU:  out = mul_product[63:32];\n+      ALU_MULHSU: out = mul_product[63:32];\n       // EX holds the instruction until the shared unit has registered its\n       // result. This leaves no combinational divide or remainder operator.\n       ALU_DIV, ALU_DIVU, ALU_REM, ALU_REMU: out = div_result;\n# Implementation notes\n\n- Replaced the signed-signed, unsigned-unsigned, and signed-unsigned 64-bit\n  products in `rtl/alu.sv` with one signed 33-by-33 product. Operand sign bits\n  are selected by the multiply opcode: both signed for MULH, only `a` signed\n  for MULHSU, and neither signed for MUL/MULHU. The ALTOPS stand-ins are\n  unchanged.\n- No deviation from the proposed RTL change.\n- Directed ALU tests exercise the real multiplier, while the standard formal\n  suite uses ALTOPS and therefore does not prove its arithmetic. Gowin timing\n  and resource impact have not been measured locally.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: pass,\n  53 passed and 0 failed. The script selected the nret=1 wrapper from\n  `core.yaml`.\n- `pytest -q cores/bench/test/test_alu.py`: pass.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: pass, 2 ELF programs tested. The plain\n  `make cosim TARGET=bench` build initially used its default `NRET=2` and\n  failed to compile because this core exposes only the nret=1 RVFI ports;\n  setting `NRET=1` matches the declared core contract.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
