{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,220p' cores/bench/implementation_notes.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Implementation notes\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a single-entry predictor with a full 32-bit branch-PC tag and target in IF.\n- EX trains it only for valid, aligned, taken conditional branches whose target is below the branch PC.\n- Registered prediction-valid and target metadata with each entering fetch PC, then carried it through IF/ID and ID/EX.\n- IF selects the registered target for a predicted successor. EX compares that target with the resolved next PC and suppresses recovery only on a match; misses and wrong predictions use the existing recovery/token path.\n\n## Deviation and timing adjustment\n\nAn initial implementation also looked up the EX recovery target in the same cycle. Gowin timing showed that extending the recovery path through the tag comparison hurt Fmax. Recovery fetches now clear prediction-valid metadata, and sequential or predicted successors refill it. This keeps the tag comparison off the recovery-target path. The prediction target register may retain irrelevant data when valid is clear.\n\n## Concerns\n\nThe predictor stores one branch, so another qualifying backedge replaces it. A taken branch is predicted after it has been learned; changing outcomes or replacement can still require EX recovery. The final worst timing path remains EX recovery to the IF PC register.\n\n## Local checks\n\n- Verilator lint: pass.\n- `RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`: 53 passed, 0 failed.\n- `NRET=1 make cosim TARGET=bench`: passed, 2 ELF programs tested. `NRET=1` matches `cores/bench/core.yaml`; the harness default of 2 does not match this core's RVFI port set.\n- `make timing TARGET=bench`: place option 0 reported Fmax 38.437 MHz, 27 logic levels, 3017 logic elements, and 763 registers.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
