ng, retirement, divide-by-zero, and signed-overflow behavior\\nprecise. This removes the divider chain from the single-cycle EX timing path;\\nCoreMark should gain Fmax, with cycle cost only if its workload executes divides.\\nPreserve the RISCV_FORMAL_ALTOPS stand-ins for formal runs.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"mediu\n…[34943 of 38015 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b75403a0e5b56f75]…\nF, with `NRET=1`.\\n- Gowin timing: address output path is from `cpu/u_ex/reg_q.effective_address`\\n  to the dmem BRAM; its reported arrival is about 4.05 ns. The new worst path\\n  is regfile to ID/EX, at 28.298 ns in placement option 0.\\n- FPGA/CoreMark eval: pass, no placement failures; median Fmax 46.082 MHz,\\n  10 iterations in 4,491,817 cycles, fitness 102.59.\\n\\n## Concern\\n\\nThe address path is substantially shorter, but the extra register bank\\nchanges placement and raises register use from 630 to 662. The current\\nplacement-option-0 Fmax is 43.472 MHz, with the regfile-to-ID/EX path now\\nlimiting timing. The full eval's median Fmax is 46.082 MHz.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T07:07:37.830858Z\", \"slot\": 0, \"round_id\": 4, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.29%): A dedicated EX effective-address path shortened the dmem address path, but its 32 added EX/MEM registers shifted placement and exposed regfile-to-ID/EX as the limiter, so local timing wins can reduce whole-core Fmax.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
