OP capture alone does not ensure a shorter EX-to-ID timing path.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -6.11%): A four-entry RAS can still regress CoreMark when canonical-return decode and EX-pop-adjusted stack-top selection feed IF's `next_pc`; even a small stack puts prediction logic on every fetch's PC path and can outweigh saved return flushes.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.29%): A dedicated EX effective-address path shortened the dmem address path, but its 32 added EX/MEM registers shifted placement and exposed regfile-to-ID/EX as the limiter, so local timing wins can reduce whole-core Fmax.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r5s0 (regression, -15.34%): Per-bit one-hot masked-OR reads expanded each 31-row flop-based register-file port into a high-fanout decode and reduction network, whose area and routing cost outweighed removing the indexed-read bypass mux.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r6s0 (regression, +2.66%): Removing `redirect` from the IF payload and `flush_if` path left EX operand/forwarding-to-dmem as the timing limiter, so shortening fetch-to-hazard feedback alone did not improve whole-core fitness.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 (placement_failed, n/a): Gowin stopped before timing because `dmem_3` exceeded the GW2AR limit at 16,384 DFFs versus 15,750, while the store queue’s unpacked address/data/mask arrays were inferred as RAMs; inspect small-FIFO memory mapping and device headroom before evaluating performance.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.62%): A four-entry shift-register RAS still cut CoreMark fitness because direct `ras0_q` removes stack indexing, but `imem_data` return decode and the `ras_top` choice remain on IF’s `next_pc` path, so predictor cost persists without EX-pop adjustment.\ncores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
