26-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -6.11%): A four-entry RAS can still regress CoreMark when canonical-return decode and EX-pop-adjusted stack-top selection feed IF's `next_pc`; even a small stack puts pre\n…[4237 of 7309 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:5c2fe71566aab47c]…\nforwarding and interlocking every ID/EX RAW match bubbles back-to-back ALU dependents; CoreMark's IPC penalty outweighed the hoped-for Fmax gain, so bypass removal must be judged against dependency frequency.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r13s2 (broken, n/a): An exact-PC IF prediction can still break `rvfi_pc_fwd` if speculative PC advance and EX mismatch recovery do not agree on the next retired PC, so validate squash-token and retirement-PC alignment for predicted branches.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r14s0 (regression, -1.70%): Priority-qualified one-hot forwarding still puts five 32-bit mask terms and an OR reduction on ID capture, so its fanout and reduction cost can outweigh removing the encoded-select stage from the EX bypass path.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r14s1 (regression, -28.05%): Moving conditional resolution into ID put forwarded-operand comparison and branch-target selection on the decode-to-fetch-PC path, whose 26.311 ns critical path erased CoreMark's saved taken-branch recovery cycle.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
