{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_1", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"sed -n '1,240p' cores/bench/LESSONS.md\"", "aggregated_output": "- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r1s0 (regression, +688.70%): A 32-step DIV/REM EX interlock overwhelmed the small timing gain in CoreMark, so count workload divide instructions before replacing a combinational operator with serialized execution.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 (improvement, +775.25%): A 32-iteration EX divider pays when removing `/` and `%` materially shortens a divide-dominated critical path and CoreMark’s rare divides keep the stall cost below the Fmax gain.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r1s2 (regression, +0.41%): Static backward-taken prediction regressed CoreMark because `imem_data` now feeds `imm_gen` and `fetch_target` before the IF PC update, so the added fetch critical path can outweigh saved EX redirect bubbles even on loop-heavy code.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r2s0 (regression, +1.93%): Explicitly sharing branch decisions still leaves separate equality and magnitude comparators plus sign-selection logic, so hand-factoring signed and unsigned comparisons can lengthen the EX-to-fetch redirect path instead of improving it.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 (regression, +4.29%): Registering EX redirects removes same-cycle operand-to-hazard feedback but adds a recovery cycle per taken branch or jump, so CoreMark's redirect frequency can outweigh the timing gain.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r2s2 (regression, -5.19%): A BTB removes `imem_data` from fetch prediction, but its indexed tag comparison and target mux still feed the IF PC update, so the lookup path can cost more timing than recurring EX redirects save.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r3s0 (regression, +1.92%): Removing `redirect` from `hazard_unit.flush_id` still leaves EX control selecting IF's NOP payload and passing through ID decode, so invalid-NOP capture alone does not ensure a shorter EX-to-ID timing path.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -6.11%): A four-entry RAS can still regress CoreMark when canonical-return decode and EX-pop-adjusted stack-top selection feed IF's `next_pc`; even a small stack puts pre\n…[2284 of 5356 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:69932b0f7052fbdf]…\nle dependencies requires youngest-writer priority and a ready-gated MEM load bypass alongside the load-use bubble.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r10s2 (regression, -19.17%): Even with the exact-PC lookup registered off IF's PC-update mux, carrying the 32-bit predicted target through IF/ID and ID/EX added pipeline state and lookup logic that one hot backedge did not repay in saved EX redirects.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r11s1 (improvement, +5.16%): Zeroing the full ID/EX bundle with a registered squash token put redirect control on EX operand and address paths; carrying the token as a sideband to suppress validity and side effects preserves the datapath and improves fitness.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r11s2 (regression, -4.38%): Compressing a BTB entry to a 12-bit target does not remove prediction cost when `if_stage` still compares the full PC tag, decodes the fetched branch, checks its immediate, and selects the predicted PC; fetch-side logic can outweigh saved EX redirects for a single hot backedge.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
