{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r3s0.yaml && git status --short'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-001-r3s0\ntitle: Remove redundant redirect hazard feedback\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  After the iterative divider removed divide logic from the critical path, timing is limited by a register-file read feeding EX branch resolution and returning through IF and hazard control to ID. The IF stage already consumes the redirect directly and emits an invalid NOP on that cycle, so sending redirect through the hazard unit to clear the same ID/IF payload adds redundant control logic to this feedback path.\nhypothesis: |\n  Remove redirect from the hazard unit's flush equations. Let IF continue to use its direct redirect input to select the target PC and present an invalid NOP to ID; keep hazard flushes for load-use bubbles and instruction-bus stalls. ID will capture the NOP on redirect, which has no side effects because it is invalid and targets x0. This should shorten the EX-to-hazard-to-ID control path without adding a recovery cycle or changing retirement behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: Remove branch redirect from flush_if and flush_id generation while preserving load-use, bus-stall, divider-busy, and data-stall behavior.\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: Disconnect the now-unneeded redirect input from hazard_unit; keep the direct EX-to-IF redirect connection.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
