n\",-3.29,46.082,[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_id\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_hazard\",\"cpu/u_id\"],\"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r4s0 (regression, -3.29%): A dedicated EX effective-address path shortened the dmem address path, but its 32 added EX/MEM registers shifted placement and exposed regfile-to-ID/EX as the limiter, so local timing wins can reduce whole-core Fmax.\",\"# Implementation notes\\n\\n## Changes\\n\\n- Added a forwarded `rs1 + imm` effective-address adder in EX.\\n- Added a dedicated EX/MEM effective-address field, written for loads and\\n  stores and cleared for other instructions. The memory stage now uses this\\n  field for `dmem_addr`, alignment checks, byte lanes, load extraction, and\\n  RVFI's aligned memory address. The general ALU result remains available for\\n  forwarding and writeback.\\n\\n## Deviation\\n\\nThe effective address has its own EX/MEM field instead of sharing\\n`alu_result`. Gowin timing with a shared result still showed the dmem BRAM\\naddress path through the general ALU result mux and multiplier logic. The\\ndedicated field isolates that path; it adds 32 registers.\\n\\n## Checks\\n\\n- Verilator lint: pass.\\n- Formal: pass, 53 checks, using the automatically selected nret=1 wrapper.\\n- Cosim: pass, both selftest and CoreMark ELF, with `NRET=1`.\\n- Gowin timing: address output path is from `cpu/u_ex/reg_q.effective_address`\\n  to the dmem BRAM; its reported arrival is about 4.05 ns. The new worst path\\n  is regfile to ID/EX, at 28.298 ns in placement option 0.\\n- FPGA/CoreMark eval: pass, no placement failures; median Fmax 46.082 MHz,\\n  10 iterations in 4,491,817 cycles, fitness 102.59.\\n\\n## Concern\\n\\nThe address path is substantially shorter, but the extra register bank\\nchanges placement and raises register use from 630 to 662. The current\\nplacement-option-0 Fmax is 43.472 MHz, with the regfile-to-ID/EX path now\\nlimiting timing. The full eval's median Fmax is 46.082 MHz.\\n\"]\n[\"hyp-20260930-001-r5s0\",\"Parallelize register-file reads\",\"regression\",-15.34,40.341,[\"clock_ibuf\",\"cpu/u_fwd\",\"cpu/u_ex\",\"cpu/u_if\",\"cpu/u_rf\"],\"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-001-r5s0 (regression, -15.34%): Per-bit one-hot masked-OR reads expanded each 31-row flop-based register-file port into a high-fanout decode and reduction network, whose area and routing cost outweighed removing the indexed-read bypass mux.\",\"\"]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The recent critical paths repeatedly run from ID operands through EX redirect logic and back into hazard/ID control. The earlier redirect experiment removed the redirect kill at ID/EX but left redirect-driven NOP substitution in fetch; I’m proposing the complementary change: keep the ID/EX kill, while removing redirect from fetch-payload substitution to shorten that feedback path."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"mkdir -p cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses && cat > cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-001-r6s0.yaml <<'EOF'\nid: hyp-20260930-001-r6s0\ntitle: \\\"Cut redirect feedback through fetch payload\\\"\ncategory: micro_opt\nmotivation: |\n  The latest timing reports show a long path from ID operands through EX branch resolution, fetch, hazard control, and back into ID. Fetch currently replaces its instruction payload with a NOP whenever EX redirects, sending the EX redirect signal through fetch data and hazard comparison logic in the same cycle.\nhypothesis: |\n  Keep redirect handling at the PC and preserve the existing redirect kill in the ID/EX register, but stop using redirect to mux the combinational fetch instruction and valid fields. The wrong-path IF/ID payload is discarded by ID/EX's redirect flush at the clock edge, so redirect need not also travel through the fetch payload into hazard detection. Continue suppressing the payload on an instruction-memory stall. This should shorten the EX-to-fetch-to-hazard feedback path without adding state or recovery cycles, improving Fmax while preserving precise control-flow behavior.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \\\"Use the fetch stall flush, rather than redirect, to gate instruction and valid outputs; retain redirect priority for the PC update.\\\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \\\"Stop asserting fetch-payload flush solely for redirect while retaining redirect-driven ID/EX kill and existing stall handling.\\\"\nEOF\n\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
