{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\"; rows=[json.loads(x) for x in open(p) if x.strip()];\nfor r in rows:\n if r.get(\\\"category\\\")==\\\"structural\\\": print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",r.get(\\\"id\\\"),r.get(\\\"outcome\\\"),\\\"fitness\\\",r.get(\\\"fitness\\\"),\\\"fmax\\\",r.get(\\\"fmax_mhz\\\"),\\\"path\\\",r.get(\\\"critical_path\\\",{}).get(\\\"modules\\\")); print((r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\") if isinstance(r.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\"),dict) else \\\"\\\")[:1300])' \"", "aggregated_output": "\n hyp-20260930-002-r1s1 improvement fitness 106.08 fmax 47.649 path ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_if', 'cpu/u_hazard', 'cpu/u_id']\nReplace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU in the EX ALU with a shared radix-2 iterative divider. Hold the divide instruction in EX and stall younger pipeline stages until its quotient or remainder is ready, while allowing other instructions to retain their current single-cycle path. This removes the long divide network from the timed combinational path and should raise Fmax with little CoreMark cycle cost; preserve RV32M corner-case behavior and the formal ALTOPS substitutions.\n\n\n hyp-20260930-002-r2s1 regression fitness 110.63 fmax 49.691 path ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']\nAdd a registered branch/jump redirect boundary: capture the resolved\nredirect target and validity in EX, then apply the redirect and squash the\nyounger EX, ID, and fetch payloads on the following cycle. This breaks the\ncurrent same-cycle operand-to-hazard-to-ID feedback path and should raise\nFmax. It adds a recovery cycle for taken branches, so retain the change only\nif the timing gain outweighs CoreMark's added control-flow cycles. Preserve\nredirect behavior across imem and dmem stalls, prevent wrong-path side\neffects, and keep branch/jump RVFI pc_wdata and retirement precise.\n\n\n hyp-20260930-002-r3s1 regression fitness 110.02 fmax 49.421 path ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']\nChange ID/EX bubble insertion so flush only clears the stage valid bit\nwhile the payload registers continue capturing their ordinary inputs.\nMake EX suppress redirects and emit inert controls whenever its input is\ninvalid, and qualify load-use detection with ID/EX valid so captured\nbubble payload cannot stall younger work. This removes the\nredirect-controlled clear mux from the wide ID/EX datapath and should\nshorten the reported register-file-to-ID feedback path without adding\nbranch recovery cycles.\nPreserve load-use replay, bus-stall holds, divid\n…[3384 of 6456 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:835ddf5ac357a060]…\nany side effect. Remove redirect\nfrom the IF payload and ID/EX flush paths while keeping the existing PC\nredirect and load-use, instruction-stall, data-stall, and divider behavior.\nThis moves the wide stage-clear and hazard feedback off the same-cycle\nEX-to-ID path without adding a recovery cycle; token generation must account\nfor stalls, invalid fetches, and a redirect target already in the PC.\n\n\n hyp-20260930-002-r9s1 regression fitness 114.08 fmax 51.17 path ['clock_ibuf', 'cpu/u_id', 'cpu/u_ex', 'cpu/u_ex/u_alu', 'cpu/u_ex']\nKeep the ID/EX datapath payload intact and carry the squash token as a separate kill sideband. Apply the kill only where an instruction can retire, redirect, access memory, write a register, or trigger load-use stalling; do not mux the forwarded operands or ALU inputs with the token. This should remove the squash-control-to-ALU dependency exposed by the latest timing report while preserving the existing no-extra-bubble redirect recovery and precise suppression of wrong-path effects.\n\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
