{"type": "item.completed", "item": {"id": "item_2", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/bash -c \"rg -n '\\\"category\\\": ?\\\"structural\\\"|structural' cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\"", "aggregated_output": "3:{\"id\": \"hyp-20260930-002-r1s1\", \"title\": \"Iterative M-extension divider\", \"category\": \"structural\", \"motivation\": \"The baseline critical path traverses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, where the 32-bit divide and remainder operators infer deep combinational logic. Those operators can dominate Fmax even though CoreMark rarely needs division.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": {\"motivation\": \"The baseline critical path traverses forwarding, EX, and the ALU, where the 32-bit divide and remainder operators infer deep combinational logic. Those operators can dominate Fmax even though CoreMark rarely needs division.\\n\", \"hypothesis\": \"Replace combinational DIV, DIVU, REM, and REMU in the EX ALU with a shared radix-2 iterative divider. Hold the divide instruction in EX and stall younger pipeline stages until its quotient or remainder is ready, while allowing other instructions to retain their current single-cycle path. This removes the long divide network from the timed combinational path and should raise Fmax with little CoreMark cycle cost; preserve RV32M corner-case behavior and the formal ALTOPS substitutions.\\n\", \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta_pct\": 15, \"confidence\": \"medium\"}, \"changes\": [{\"file\": \"rtl/alu.sv\", \"description\": \"Remove combinational divide/remainder operators and route those operations through a shared iterative unit, preserving ALTOPS behavior and RV32M corner cases.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/divider.sv\", \"description\": \"Add a radix-2 iterative signed/unsigned divide-remainder unit with a ready/done interface.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/ex_stage.sv\", \"description\": \"Start and wait for divide operations, hold their EX/MEM payload until completion, and use the divider result.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/hazard_unit.sv\", \"description\": \"Propagate the execute-unit busy condition as a pipeline stall without duplicating retirement or memory side effects.\"}, {\"file\": \"rtl/core.sv\", \"description\": \"Wire divider busy/valid control into pipeline hazard handling while retaining the existing top-level I/O contract.\"}]}, \"expected_impact\": {\"fitness_delta\n…[51868 of 54940 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:b42edac45bc46c34]…\ntor lint: passed with the requested command.\\n- Formal: passed, 53 checks and 0 failures (`RTL_DIR=cores/bench/rtl\\n  CORE_NAME=bench bash formal/run_all.sh`).\\n- Cosim: passed both ELF tests with `NRET=1`. The initial unqualified\\n  `make cosim TARGET=bench` build defaulted to NRET=2 and failed because this\\n  core exposes only the NRET=1 RVFI ports; rerunning with `NRET=1` passed.\\n- Gowin timing: `make timing TARGET=bench` completed; place option 0 reported\\n  47.581 MHz, 17 logic levels, and 2967 LUTs. The longest path was 26.311 ns\\n  from the ID/EX immediate through EX/ID/IF to the fetch PC. The added\\n  decode-to-PC logic remains a timing concern.\\n\", \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-30T11:47:08.476506Z\", \"slot\": 1, \"round_id\": 14, \"lesson\": \"- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r14s1 (regression, -28.05%): Moving conditional resolution into ID put forwarded-operand comparison and branch-target selection on the decode-to-fetch-PC path, whose 26.311 ns critical path erased CoreMark's saved taken-branch recovery cycle.\"}\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
