{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"python3 -c 'import json; xs=[json.loads(x) for x in open(\\\"cores/bench/experiments/log.jsonl\\\") if x.strip()];\nfor x in xs:\n if x.get(\\\"id\\\") in (\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r6s1\\\",\\\"hyp-20260930-002-r7s1\\\"):\n  print(x[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"err\\\",x.get(\\\"error\\\"),\\\"notes\\\",x.get(\\\"implementation_notes\\\",\\\"\\\")[:2500],\\\"hyp\\\",x.get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",{}).get(\\\"hypothesis\\\",\\\"\\\")[:900])'\"","aggregated_output":"hyp-20260930-002-r6s1 err placement_failed notes  hyp Add a small in-order store buffer between MEM and the external data bus.\nEnqueue ordinary RAM stores when they reach MEM and let younger instructions\nproceed while the buffer drains, stalling only when it is full. Keep loads\nordered by waiting for older buffered stores to drain before issuing them,\nand keep MMIO writes synchronous so the CoreMark timing markers and UART\nretain their ordering. Holding each queued request stable until io_dmemReady\nshould hide isolated write stalls and improve CoreMark IPC; the main risk is\nqueue and hazard-control logic offsetting that gain in area or timing.\n\nhyp-20260930-002-r7s1 err placement_failed notes  hyp Add a single-entry store buffer using explicit scalar address, data, and mask registers. Accept ordinary RAM stores into the buffer so younger independent instructions can execute while the external request waits, and stall only when the buffer is full or ordering requires it. Keep loads ordered behind buffered stores and keep MMIO writes synchronous; retire stores in order when their buffered request is accepted into the committed buffer. A scalar entry should hide some dmem stalls without triggering the queue-array mapping that exhausted device registers.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
