{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r5s1.yaml && git status --short -- cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r5s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r5s1\ntitle: \"Decouple fetch with a small instruction queue\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  The timed workload injects independent random instruction-memory stalls, and\n  the current fetch stage turns each unavailable response into a pipeline\n  bubble. Data-memory waits and other frontend holds also leave opportunities\n  to fetch sequential instructions before decode needs them.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a small FIFO between instruction fetch and decode, with each entry\n  carrying its instruction PC and word. Let fetch continue filling the FIFO\n  while decode is held by downstream hazards or data-memory backpressure, and\n  let decode consume queued instructions when io_imemReady is low. This should\n  hide some later instruction-bus stalls and improve CoreMark iterations per\n  cycle without putting a predictor lookup on the fetch-address path. On an EX\n  redirect, atomically discard younger queued entries and restart fetch at the\n  redirect target; preserve response/PC alignment, bus-ready semantics, and\n  precise retirement through simultaneous stalls and redirects.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 6\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a shallow PC-tagged fetch FIFO, continue sequential fetch during downstream holds, and restart/flush it correctly on redirects.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Separate fetch response stalls from decode consumption so queued instructions can advance while the imem bus is stalled.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire fetch FIFO valid/consume and downstream hold controls while preserving the existing top-level and RVFI contract.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
