{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r7s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r7s1\ntitle: \"Scalar one-entry store buffer\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  Random data-bus stalls currently freeze the whole pipeline for a pending store, even when younger instructions do not need memory. The prior store-buffer attempt failed placement because its unpacked queue arrays were inferred as large RAM structures on this FPGA.\nhypothesis: |\n  Add a single-entry store buffer using explicit scalar address, data, and mask registers. Accept ordinary RAM stores into the buffer so younger independent instructions can execute while the external request waits, and stall only when the buffer is full or ordering requires it. Keep loads ordered behind buffered stores and keep MMIO writes synchronous; retire stores in order when their buffered request is accepted into the committed buffer. A scalar entry should hide some dmem stalls without triggering the queue-array mapping that exhausted device registers.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 7\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/mem_stage.sv\n    description: \"Add a one-entry store buffer with explicit scalar flops, stable bus draining, and ordered handling for loads and MMIO.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Allow younger non-memory instructions to proceed while a buffered RAM store drains; stall on buffer-full and memory-order conflicts.\"\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Wire store-buffer status and request routing while preserving in-order retirement and the existing core interface.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
