{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c 'cat cores/bench/experiments/hypotheses/hyp-20260930-002-r8s1.yaml'","aggregated_output":"id: hyp-20260930-002-r8s1\ntitle: \"Defer branch squash with a registered token\"\ncategory: structural\nmotivation: |\n  After divider serialization, the measured timing path still crosses EX branch\n  resolution, fetch redirect handling, hazard control, and ID capture. The\n  earlier valid-only bubble change improved Fmax but remained just below the\n  tournament acceptance threshold. Branch recovery bubbles also reduced the\n  gain when redirect decisions were simply registered.\nhypothesis: |\n  Let IF/ID capture the sequential fetch normally on the cycle EX resolves a\n  branch, and record a one-cycle squash token when that fetched instruction is\n  actually admitted to ID/EX. Mask the ID/EX bundle with this registered token\n  before it reaches execute, forwarding, or hazard logic, so the wrong-path\n  instruction becomes an inert bubble before any side effect. Remove redirect\n  from the IF payload and ID/EX flush paths while keeping the existing PC\n  redirect and load-use, instruction-stall, data-stall, and divider behavior.\n  This moves the wide stage-clear and hazard feedback off the same-cycle\n  EX-to-ID path without adding a recovery cycle; token generation must account\n  for stalls, invalid fetches, and a redirect target already in the PC.\nexpected_impact:\n  fitness_delta_pct: 8\n  confidence: medium\nchanges:\n  - file: rtl/core.sv\n    description: \"Track whether a redirect-adjacent fetch entered ID/EX and mask that bundle as an inert bubble before EX, forwarding, and hazard consumers.\"\n  - file: rtl/if_stage.sv\n    description: \"Allow the sequential fetch payload to enter ID/EX during redirect resolution while preserving PC redirect priority and imem-stall invalidation.\"\n  - file: rtl/hazard_unit.sv\n    description: \"Stop using EX redirect as an IF/ID