26-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r3s2 (regression, -6.11%): A four-entry RAS can still regress CoreMark when canonical-return decode and EX-pop-adjusted stack-top selection feed IF's `next_pc`; even a small stack puts pre\n…[1072 of 4144 chars elided by tools/bench/transcript.py; sha256:d38858932400ae80]…\ness/data/mask arrays were inferred as RAMs; inspect small-FIFO memory mapping and device headroom before evaluating performance.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r6s2 (regression, -8.62%): A four-entry shift-register RAS still cut CoreMark fitness because direct `ras0_q` removes stack indexing, but `imem_data` return decode and the `ras_top` choice remain on IF’s `next_pc` path, so predictor cost persists without EX-pop adjustment.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-003-r7s2 (regression, -20.03%): A one-entry 11-bit backedge predictor still regressed CoreMark because PC-tag-only hits can send aliased non-branches to costly EX recovery, while the tag compare and target mux remain on every IF PC update.\n- 2026-09-30 hyp-20260930-002-r8s1 (improvement, +4.93%): Registering a squash token for the redirect-cycle instruction admitted from IF/ID and masking ID/EX before EX and hazard consumers shortens EX-to-ID feedback without adding a recovery bubble; held branches must suppress repeat redirects so the target fetch survives.\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
